  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A={\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma }$ defined as \n\n$$\nR={(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),(\\beta ,\\beta ),(\\beta ,\\alpha ),(\\alpha ,\\beta )}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $R$ is reflexive\n- $R$ is symmetric\n- $R$ is transitive\n- $R$ is an equivalence relation",
  "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* reflexive because $(\\gamma ,\\gamma )\\notin R.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in {\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma },$ if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,z)\\in R,$ then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Recall that it's sufficient to check only the cases when both pairs have different components: \n\n- $(\\alpha ,\\beta )\\in R$ and $(\\beta ,\\alpha )\\in R,$ and also $(\\alpha ,\\alpha )\\in R$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $(\\beta ,\\alpha )\\in R$ and $(\\alpha ,\\beta )\\in R,$ and also $(\\beta ,\\beta )\\in R$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation is *not* an equivalence relation since it's not reflexive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
